\DOC X86_SINGLE_STEP_TAC

\TYPE {X86_SINGLE_STEP_TAC : thm * (thm option array) -> string -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes one x86 instruction of the program described by the
supplied execution theorem, advancing the goal's {eventually} state from the
current state variable to a new one named by the string argument.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument {th : thm * (thm option array)} is an execution record
pairing a theorem giving the length of the code list with a decoding cache; the
instructions stepped are those of that theorem's code list. The second argument
{s : string} names the state variable to introduce for the post-instruction
state. Applied to a goal whose conclusion is {eventually x86 (\s'. ...) t},
with {t} the current state variable, the tactic leaves one subgoal with the
same conclusion except that {t} is replaced by a fresh state variable {u} named
by {s}; the body {P} of the {eventually} predicate is untouched. Every
assumption mentioning the old state ({bytes_loaded}, component reads, and the
{MAYCHANGE} frame relating the initial state to the current one) is discharged
and replaced by the corresponding assumption about the new state: components
the instruction does not affect keep their values, components it writes get new
reads (program counter advanced, destination registers, memory cells, flags,
event traces), and the {MAYCHANGE} term grows by the components touched.
Assumptions that are pure arithmetic or side conditions not mentioning a state
are generally carried over unchanged, though their order may change; new
arithmetic assumptions describing the instruction's result may be added, for
example carry/sum relations, or {x1 ... xn} with n possibly zero.
{
      A ?- eventually x86 P t
   ========================== X86_SINGLE_STEP_TAC th s
      A' ?- eventually x86 P u
}
\noindent where {A'} is the restated assumption list, about {u}.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-goal, single-step behaviour, always
on goals of this {eventually} shape.

\ENDDOC
